@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-inject-a-now-clock-from-outside-the-domain-for-task-timestamps
+type: decision-log
+title: "Inject a now() clock from outside the domain for task timestamps"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks needed `createdAt` and `updatedAt`, ISO-8601 date-time strings set to the actual time an add or a
+change happens. `src/domain/` must not read the clock (`r3-no-clock-or-randomness`: no `Date.now()`, no
+bare `new Date()`), which rules out having `task.ts` or `list.ts` — both under `src/domain/` — read the
+time themselves.
+
+This mirrors the id problem solved in `dl-001`: there, the fix was a counter kept entirely inside the
+domain, because a counter is neither a clock nor randomness. A timestamp has no such escape — the whole
+point is that it reflects real wall-clock time, so the actual read of `Date` has to happen somewhere the
+directive does not reach, and the result has to be handed into the domain from outside it.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `src/domain/task.ts`'s functions (`createTask`, `completeTask`, `reopenTask`, `renameTask`, `addTag`,
+  `removeTag`) take `now` as a plain `string` parameter: an already-formatted ISO-8601 value they stamp
+  onto `createdAt`/`updatedAt`. They never construct a `Date` themselves.
+- `src/domain/list.ts`'s `createTaskList` takes `now: () => string` as a mandatory parameter (no
+  default): a function it calls at the moment of every add or change, threading its result into the
+  `task.ts` calls above. Mandatory, not optional, because any in-domain default would itself have to
+  read the clock to be useful, which is exactly what the directive forbids.
+- `src/index.ts`'s `createTodoList` — outside `src/domain/`, so the directive does not apply — supplies
+  the real clock as a default: `now: () => string = () => new Date().toISOString()`. Callers of the
+  public API keep working with no changes; tests (and any future caller) can override it.
+- Every successful call to `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag` or `untag` bumps `updatedAt`, even when
+  the operation is a no-op in content (renaming a task to the title it already has, tagging with a tag
+  it already carries). The request describes these five operations themselves as "a change"; the
+  existing code already does not special-case their no-op forms (e.g. `addTag` always decides via
+  `unique(...)`, not via an "is this tag new" check), so adding a content-equality check here would be
+  new, speculative complexity for a case nobody asked to distinguish.
+- Tests get their own deterministic stand-in clock (`fakeClock()` in `test/list.test.ts`): a closure over
+  a tick counter turned into an ISO string via `new Date(++ticks * 1000).toISOString()`. This is
+  deterministic construction from an explicit number, not a clock read, so it satisfies the testing
+  directive's ban on wall-clock reliance; it lives in `test/`, outside `src/domain/`, so `r3` does not
+  apply to it either way.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `Task.createdAt` survives every transform automatically, the same way `Task.id` already did, because
+  every transform still returns a `{ ...task, ... }` shallow copy that only overrides `updatedAt`.
+- The domain's public surface grew a parameter on six functions; every call site in `list.ts` already
+  needed updating for `dl-001`'s id plumbing, so the shape of the change (thread one more value through
+  the same call sites) was cheap.
+- No new dependency was needed (`r1-no-new-dependency`); no `Date`/`Math.random` call was added under
+  `src/domain/` (`r3-no-clock-or-randomness`).
